      "answer": "At revision `6d34e3e130e6e0b25299d524f5a04f7fd2f83f1d`, assuming the records and batches remain unchanged and the timeout raises `TimeoutError`, `gateway.deliver` makes four calls:\n\n1. Send records 1 and 2. The call times out.\n2. Retry the same batch of records 1 and 2. It returns normally.\n3. Send records 3 and 4. It returns normally.\n4. Send record 5. It returns normally.\n\nThe batch size is two, each batch permits up to three attempts, and a normal callback return ends that batch's retry loop. The retry has no delay or backoff. See [gateway.py:1](/tmp/juan-standalone-1893x9jy/how/fixture/gateway.py:1) and [gateway.py:6](/tmp/juan-standalone-1893x9jy/how/fixture/gateway.py:6), lines 6 through 14.\n\nIt then returns **5**, the value of `len(records)` at return time. This establishes that every batch reached a callback call that returned normally. The callback's return value is ignored. It does not establish remote receipt, persistence, or exactly-once delivery. The first timed-out call might already have taken effect, so retrying it could duplicate effects. The fixture provides no callback implementation to resolve that uncertainty. See [gateway.py:10](/tmp/juan-standalone-1893x9jy/how/fixture/gateway.py:10), [gateway.py:15](/tmp/juan-standalone-1893x9jy/how/fixture/gateway.py:15), and [README.md:3](/tmp/juan-standalone-1893x9jy/how/fixture/README.md:3).\n\nA third consecutive timeout for any batch would propagate and stop delivery before later batches; other exceptions propagate immediately. This answer comes from static source inspection. No fixture code or tests were executed.\n\nThe direct `how` invocation completed using one configured explainer. It did not activate juan-mode.\n",
