      "answer": "The strongest record says the change to **two records was for a claimed partner request limit, not a throughput benchmark**. Commit `e896cb90d9dcd61420426088f58d4429abbb86cc` changes only `BATCH_SIZE = 3` to `2` and explicitly says to keep the retry count unchanged. This is **direct evidence of the stated rationale**, not independent proof of the partner contract.\n\nThere is an unresolved contradiction: [docs/decision.md:3](/tmp/juan-standalone-1893x9jy/why/fixture/docs/decision.md:3) says there is no record-count limit and attributes two-record batches only to latency reduction, with no measurements. That document already existed when the initial code used a batch size of three. The change-specific commit is stronger evidence for why the edit happened, but the local records do not settle the actual limit. Neither record establishes a measured throughput optimization.\n\n**Why exactly three attempts is unknown.** The initial commit `5dfd6b0e22169c7107175e592cc0cd9279fe42c6` introduced that number without explanation. The later instruction explains why it was preserved during the batching change, not why it was originally selected. [gateway.py:8](/tmp/juan-standalone-1893x9jy/why/fixture/gateway.py:8) allows three total calls per batch\u2014one initial attempt and at most two retries\u2014only after `TimeoutError`. Success stops retrying; the third timeout and other exceptions propagate. This behavior was read, not executed.\n\nRecommendations before changing it:\n\n- **Preserve:** provisionally retain the two-record maximum until the conflicting contract claims are reconciled. Keep three total attempts separate from batching changes. Preserve or explicitly revise ordered sequential batches, the short final batch, timeout-only retries, stop-on-success, error propagation, stopping after a failed batch, and the successful return count ([gateway.py:5](/tmp/juan-standalone-1893x9jy/why/fixture/gateway.py:5)).\n- **Establish before changing:** the authoritative request-size contract, caller expectations, timeout semantics and idempotency. Obtain measurements before making a performance claim, then align code and documentation.\n- **Avoid:** treating three attempts as three retries, presenting throughput as the established motivation, or silently dismissing the contradictory document.\n- **Inferred risks:** exceeding two could violate the recorded cap if accurate; replaying a batch after a timeout could duplicate an already-performed side effect. Neither exactly-once delivery nor safe retries are established.\n\nCoverage of the authorized sources:\n\n- **Source control \u2014 searched:** all three commits, full bodies/patches, rename-following, blame, and introduction/incident searches. `6d34e3e130e6e0b25299d524f5a04f7fd2f83f1d` is a pure rename from `send.py` to `gateway.py`. Incident/postmortem message searches found no records.\n- **Issues and tickets \u2014 unavailable:** no local records or references found; external systems outside scope.\n- **Long-form documents \u2014 searched:** both tracked documents, README and decision, read fully; no retry rationale or measurements found.\n- **Team chat \u2014 outside authorized scope:** no local archive; no external chat queried.\n- **Infrastructure observability \u2014 unavailable:** no local incident, trace or metric records; [README.md:3](/tmp/juan-standalone-1893x9jy/why/fixture/README.md:3) says no external services belong to the fixture.\n- **Error tracking \u2014 unavailable:** no local event records; external services outside scope.\n- **Product analytics \u2014 unavailable:** no local experiment or measurement records; decision explicitly supplies none.\n\nInspected revision: `6d34e3e130e6e0b25299d524f5a04f7fd2f83f1d`. All three commits carry 2026-10-11 23:24:45 UTC; ancestry establishes their order. Investigation completed within local scope using two configured investigators and one configured synthesizer, all terminal with no pending child runs. Coordinator